WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.108
An endpoint is a device that connects to an organization's environment.

00:00:04.108 --> 00:00:08.116
Laptops, desktops, servers, and virtual machines are all examples of endpoints.

00:00:08.116 --> 00:00:12.489
These systems matter because they are where people work, files are opened,

00:00:12.489 --> 00:00:15.040
credentials are used, and business applications run.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.412
Attackers often target endpoints because a compromised endpoint can become a foothold.

00:00:19.412 --> 00:00:24.149
From there, an attacker may steal data, install malware, capture credentials, move laterally,

00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:25.607
or reach other systems.

00:00:25.607 --> 00:00:28.522
Even when strong network and identity controls exist,

00:00:28.522 --> 00:00:32.530
endpoint activity can reveal what is actually happening on the device.

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:34.716
Endpoint Detection and Response, or EDR,

00:00:34.716 --> 00:00:39.089
exists because organizations need visibility into these systems and a way to

00:00:39.089 --> 00:00:40.911
respond when suspicious activity appears.

00:00:40.911 --> 00:00:43.097
EDR is not a magic shield.

00:00:43.097 --> 00:00:47.469
It is a visibility and response layer that helps defenders understand endpoint

00:00:47.469 --> 00:00:50.020
behavior and act when something looks wrong.
